      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "Given that $r(t)=⟨{cos}^{2}⁡t,\\,{sin}^{2}⁡t⟩,$ calculate $r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$",
      "worked_solution": "We substitute $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ into our function, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2},\\,{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
